(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-htn-beta-blockers-quiz"]={"title":"Beta Blockers Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"In thyrotoxicosis, beta blockers relieve tachycardia, tremor and anxiety. What additional effect do they have on thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3)?","choices":["Less thyroid hormone synthesis","More thyroid-stimulating hormone","Less peripheral T4-to-T3 conversion","Less iodine uptake by the gland"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. They reduce the peripheral conversion of thyroxine (T4) to the more active triiodothyronine (T3), besides blunting the adrenergic symptoms. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which conduction problem can beta blockers cause?","choices":["Reflex tachycardia","Atrioventricular block","Supraventricular tachycardia","Ventricular tachycardia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cardiodepressive effects include bradycardia (heart rate under 60) and first-, second- or third-degree atrioventricular block, as well as negative inotropy and fatigue. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"How do beta blockers lower angiotensin II?","choices":["Blocking renal beta-1 receptors","Inhibiting the converting enzyme","Blocking the AT1 receptor","Blocking lung beta-2 receptors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Beta-1 receptors on the juxtaglomerular cells mediate renin release, so blocking them reduces renin and therefore angiotensin II. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which beta-1 selective blocker is given intravenously?","choices":["Esmolol","Atenolol","Bisoprolol","Acebutolol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Esmolol is the second-generation, beta-1 selective agent marked for intravenous use. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which beta blocker is beta-1 selective?","choices":["Atenolol","Propranolol","Nadolol","Timolol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Atenolol is a second-generation, beta-1 selective agent, alongside acebutolol, bisoprolol, esmolol and metoprolol. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A patient wants to stop his metoprolol abruptly before a trip. What is the danger?","choices":["Hypoglycemia, sweating or confusion","Angina, infarction or a pressure surge","Bronchospasm, wheeze or cough","Hyperkalemia, weakness or palpitations"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sudden withdrawal can cause acute angina, myocardial infarction or a marked rise in blood pressure, so the drug is withdrawn slowly. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"A patient on propranolol develops vivid nightmares and low mood. Which property of the drug explains this?","choices":["Beta-1 selectivity","Alpha-1 blockade","Intravenous use","High lipid solubility"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Highly lipid-soluble beta blockers such as propranolol cause more central effects: depression, nightmares and vivid dreams, hallucinations and fatigue. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which beta blocker is indicated for heart failure?","choices":["Bisoprolol","Propranolol","Atenolol","Nadolol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The three beta blockers used for heart failure are carvedilol, metoprolol succinate and bisoprolol. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"A 61-year-old with hypertension had a myocardial infarction last year. Which class should generally be used initially?","choices":["An alpha-1 blocker","A beta blocker","Hydralazine","Clonidine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A history of myocardial infarction is a reason to start a beta blocker early; beta blockade protects ischemic myocardium and protects against recurrent infarction. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"How does beta blockade help a patient with exertional angina?","choices":["It dilates the coronary arteries","It raises myocardial contractility","It dissolves coronary thrombus","It prolongs time to symptoms"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. By blunting the rise in heart rate and contractility, beta blockade lengthens the time to the angina-provoking heart rate and prolongs exercise time. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which beta blocker also blocks alpha-1 receptors?","choices":["Metoprolol","Carvedilol","Atenolol","Propranolol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Carvedilol and labetalol are the third-generation exceptions that add alpha-1 blockade; carvedilol is also antioxidant and antiproliferative. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Why are beta blockers contraindicated in a patient with COPD (chronic obstructive pulmonary disease) who has bronchospasm?","choices":["They cause bradykinin-induced cough","They cause sodium and water retention","They block airway beta-2 receptors","They raise blood carbon dioxide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Blocking pulmonary beta-2 receptors reduces bronchial smooth muscle relaxation; about a third of COPD patients develop bronchospasm. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"A patient with type 1 diabetes is starting propranolol. What should he be told about hypoglycemia?","choices":["It will become less frequent","Insulin will no longer be needed","Warning signs may be masked","Blood sugar will run high only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers inhibit glycogenolysis, so hypoglycemia lasts longer, and they mask its adrenergic warning symptoms. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"How should a beta blocker be started in a patient with heart failure?","choices":["Full dose from the first day","Only once symptoms have gone","Intravenously for the first week","Very low dose, increased slowly"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers initially worsen heart failure symptoms, so they are started at a very low dose and increased slowly. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 70"}]};
